# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# macOS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Finder writes .DS_Store into every directory it displays, recording view
# settings (icon positions, window size, sort order). Harmless locally, but it
# also embeds the directory listing — including names of since-deleted files —
# which is why it is treated as an information disclosure finding when it ends
# up on a web server.
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Windows
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Thumbs.db
Thumbs.db:encryptable
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Linux
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*~
.fuse_hidden*
.nfs*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editors
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*.bak
*.tmp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Working notes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Completed checklists are audit evidence and may name findings in a live
# estate. Keep filled-in copies out of the repo unless that is deliberate.
*-completed.md
*-filled.md
/scratch/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Go
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Only if someone builds the script rather than `go run`-ing it.
/compliance-report
/audit-run

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Audit output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Audit results ARE committed here — this repository is private and holds the
# engagement's evidence alongside the tooling that produced it.
#
# What that means in practice: results name every public bucket, every
# over-permissioned account, and every open firewall rule in the estate. Repo
# access is therefore access to the findings, so keep the access list matched
# to who should see them. If this repository is ever made public, forked, or
# moved between organizations, the results go with it — git history outlives
# the private setting.
#
# Only build artefacts and scratch files are ignored:
audit-run.log
*.cis.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Terraform
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# State records the audit identity and every binding it created. It is the
# teardown record and must not be committed.
#
# .terraform/ holds downloaded provider binaries — the google provider alone is
# ~116 MB, over GitHub's 100 MB per-file limit. Never commit it.
#
# terraform.tfvars IS committed: it holds an organization ID, a project ID and
# a list of auditor emails. None of those are credentials, and committing it
# means anyone can reproduce exactly what was applied.
**/.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
tfplan
crash.log

# .terraform.lock.hcl SHOULD be committed — it pins provider versions so every
# operator resolves the same ones. It is small and contains no secrets.
!**/.terraform.lock.hcl

# Audit output names real projects, buckets and IAM principals.
#
# In a CUSTOMER's private engagement repository the results ARE committed —
# that is the deliverable, and a finding should be reproducible from the
# settings that produced it. DELETE THE NEXT LINE on the customer's side.
#
# In THIS repository — public, and the kit's own development — they are not.
# Two accidents have already put a live IAM inventory in this history, both
# from `git add -A` sweeping a directory nobody meant to commit.
/audit-state/
#
# Nothing in this repository should ever hold a credential. Service account
# keys in particular: the audit runs by impersonation precisely so that no key
# exists, and committing one would break safeguard 5.2 — which this repository
# exists to test.
*.json.key
*-key.json
credentials.json
service-account*.json

# Settings are the auditor's own: the customer's project names, registry
# paths and residency policy. The examples and the readme are committed so a
# fresh clone knows what to fill in; the filled-in files are not.
/config/*
!/config/readme.md
!/config/*.example
